    ('Classical Chinese (Wényán)', 'Causality is not forced morphologically but emerges topologically from parallelism and symmetry', 'Pure relational constellation in the now', 'Language', 'Sino-Tibetan / context-dependent functional fluidity', 'No inflection, no gender, no explicit tense; a character can be noun, verb or adjective', 'whether the relation between the parts is stated or left to the arrangement', 'connective', '["isolating", "fluidity", "parallelism"]', '["Coexistence", "Causality"]', 'Pulleyblank, Outline of Classical Chinese Grammar (1995); Zadrapa, Word-Class Flexibility in Classical Chinese (Brill)', 'sourced'),
