    ('Pirahã', 'Pushes toward restriction to the immediately perceivable; how far it rules out hypothetical nesting is exactly what is disputed', 'Absolute empirical present', 'Language', 'Mura family / radical grammatical immediacy', '*hói* and *hoí* are "small quantity" and "larger quantity" rather than numerals, and a corpus search finds no sentential or NP embedding (Piantadosi et al. 2016); both claims are contested (Nevins, Pesetsky & Rodrigues 2009). The colour-term claim of the same debate is the weakest of the three and is not carried here', 'whether the content is immediately perceivable', 'whole passage', '["immediacy", "empirical", "recursion", "quantity"]', '["Time & aspect"]', 'Everett (2005); contra Nevins, Pesetsky & Rodrigues (2009); corpus: Piantadosi et al., PLOS ONE (2016)', 'contested'),
    ('Tz''utujil', 'To say a thing is done, the clause gives up its verb: completion is not asserted of the event but predicated of the participant, which is why a finished action and a state of the world are not two readings of one form here', 'Completion predicated of the thing, not of the event', 'Language', 'Mayan (K''ichean) / the perfect as a non-verbal predicate', 'Aspect runs on x- (completive), k-/n- (incompletive) and the aspectual predicate *tajin* (progressive); the perfect leaves the verbal system altogether — Duncan analyses it as a deverbal participial adjective or a possessed nominal that still carries two argument roles. Whether x-/k- are aspect at all is disputed: Bešlin reanalyses K''iche'' as tensed', 'whether completion is predicated of the event or of the thing', 'verb', '["aspect", "perfect", "non-verbal-predicate", "completion"]', '["Time & aspect"]', 'Dayley, Tzutujil Grammar (Berkeley diss. 1981; UC Press 1985); Duncan, On the perfect tense-aspect in K''ichee''an Mayan (2016); contra the tenseless reading: Bešlin on K''iche'' as a tensed language', 'contested'),
    ('Kwakiutl (Kwak''wala)', 'Substances cannot be thought without their visibility status', 'Spatial visibility of the information object', 'Language', 'Wakashan / obligatory epistemic localization', 'For every object it must be carried whether it is visible/invisible and near the speaker or the hearer', 'whether the thing is visible and to whom', 'noun', '["visibility", "deixis", "localization"]', '["Evidence & certainty", "Space & orientation"]', 'Boas, Kwakiutl Grammar with a Glossary of the Suffixes (1947); Chung, The internal structure of Kwakwala nominal domain (2007)', 'sourced'),
    ('Hua (Yagaria)', 'A chain cannot be built forward blindly: every link states in advance whose act follows, so the sequence of participants is fixed before the sentence reaches them', 'Who is coming, settled before the clause closes', 'Language', 'Trans-New Guinea / anticipatory switch-reference on medial verbs', 'A medial verb carries not only whether the subject changes but the person and number of the *upcoming* one: the anticipatory marker names who acts next, so a clause cannot be closed before the following clause''s subject is settled. Haiman separates coordinate from subordinate medials as well, the latter presupposed rather than asserted', 'who the next subject will be', 'connective', '["switch-reference", "anticipatory", "medial-verb", "clause-chaining"]', '["Rank & salience", "Coexistence"]', 'Haiman, Hua: A Papuan Language of the Eastern Highlands of New Guinea (1980)', 'sourced'),
    ('Nganasan', 'Denial cannot be appended to a finished clause: it is decided beforehand whether an event, a constituent or an existence is being denied, and the finite marking moves onto the denial to carry it', 'Denial as the finite element, not as an operator on it', 'Language', 'Samoyedic (Uralic) / negation as an inflected auxiliary', 'Verbal negation runs through the auxiliary *ńi-*, which takes the inflection the main verb gives up; the lexical verb drops into the connegative and keeps only its derivation. Two further negators divide the field: *ńintuu* negates a constituent and attributive predications, *ďaŋku* the existential and possessive ones — Gusev', 'what the denial is aimed at', 'verb', '["negation", "negative-auxiliary", "connegative", "existential"]', '["Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Gusev, Negation in Nganasan (Negation in Uralic Languages, TSL 108); Wagner-Nagy, A Grammar of Nganasan (2018)', 'sourced'),
    ('Inuktitut', 'Prevents the blurring of contexts in spatial references', 'Fragmentation of space and materiality', 'Language', 'Eskimo-Aleut / locative and phenomenological classes', 'No abstract umbrella terms for space; forces whether an open unbounded space or a closed 3D body', 'whether the space is open or a closed body', 'noun', '["space", "materiality", "classes"]', '["Space & orientation", "Object boundaries"]', 'Dorais, The Language of the Inuit: Syntax, Semantics and Society in the Arctic (2010); Johns, on incorporation in Inuktitut', 'sourced'),
