    ('Ojibwe (obviation)', 'Forces one discourse-wide decision about whose account this is and marks every mention of the others as secondary, so a report cannot drift silently between the parties it speaks about', 'Which of several third parties the account is about', 'Language', 'Algonquian family / proximate-obviative marking', 'Among animate third persons exactly one is proximate and stays unmarked while every other one carries the obviative suffix for as long as the passage runs; "John and Bill were friends ever since he first saw him" cannot be formed without settling who saw whom', 'which of several third parties the account is about', 'person', '["obviation", "proximate", "obviative", "salience", "discourse-scope", "third-person"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Valentine, Nishnaabemwin Reference Grammar (2001)', 'sourced'),
    ('Walbiri (Warlpiri)', 'Frees thinking from sequential linearity and reads sentences as associative clusters', 'Non-hierarchical, network-like arrangement of information', 'Language', 'Pama-Nyungan / radical syntactic freedom (scrambling)', 'Nominals scatter freely and carry their function in their case suffixes rather than in their position; only the auxiliary is fixed, in second place', 'whether the sequence carries any function', 'word order', '["scrambling", "free-word-order", "network"]', '["Coexistence", "Rank & salience"]', 'Hale, Warlpiri and the Grammar of Non-Configurational Languages (1983); Simpson, Warlpiri Morpho-Syntax (1991)', 'sourced'),
    ('Thai', 'No statement without first balancing the relational structure of those involved', 'Exact sociometric position in the social web', 'Language', 'Kra-Dai / open-ended relational person reference', 'Person reference is an open set rather than a pronoun list: dozens of terms for "I" and "you" by age, status, kinship and emotional distance, and kinship words serve as pronouns between people who are not related', 'the social position of the participants to each other', 'person', '["pronoun", "status", "social"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Rank & salience"]', 'Iwasaki & Ingkaphirom, A Reference Grammar of Thai', 'sourced'),
