    ('Sanskrit (Syādvāda)', 'Leads out of the binary true/false tunnel and allows contradictory system states at once', 'Multidimensional relativity of truth', 'Language', 'Indian logic philosophy (Jaina) / sevenfold predication', 'Knows seven truth values ("maybe it is", "maybe it is not", "maybe indescribable" …)', 'which of seven truth values the statement holds', 'whole passage', '["logic", "syadvada", "many-valued"]', '["Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Jaina saptabhaṅgī; Pragati Jain, Saptabhaṅgī: The Jaina Theory of Sevenfold Predication', 'sourced'),
    ('Hindi-Urdu (vector verbs)', 'Forces stating not only that an action is finished but whom its outcome falls to and how it came about', 'Direction of benefit and manner of completion', 'Language', 'Indo-Aryan / explicator compound verbs', 'A semantically bleached second verb attaches to the main verb: kar denā (done for another), kar lenā (done for oneself), kar baiṭhnā (done inadvertently), kar ḍālnā (done forcefully and irreversibly)', 'whom the outcome falls to and how it came about', 'verb', '["vector-verb", "benefactive", "completion", "disposition"]', '["Time & aspect", "Agency & control"]', 'Hook, The Compound Verb in Hindi (University of Michigan, 1974)', 'sourced'),
    ('Turkish (stacked causatives)', 'Forces stating how many links stand between the one who speaks and the one who acted, so a delegated act cannot be reported as a first-degree event', 'Depth of the delegation chain', 'Language', 'Turkic / recursive causative stacking', 'The causative suffix reapplies to its own output: yap (do), yaptır (have done), yaptırt (have someone have it done), yaptırttır (one link further). The forms are not in doubt; whether each added suffix also adds a causing event is — Key reads the stacked forms as morphological reduplication over one event, the reply in Linguistic Inquiry as genuine recursion, and only the second reading carries this lens', 'how many instigators stand between speaker and act', 'verb', '["causative", "stacking", "delegation", "recursion", "agent-chain"]', '["Causality", "Agency & control"]', 'Göksel & Kerslake, Turkish: A Comprehensive Grammar (2005); on whether stacking really adds a causing event: Key (2013) and the reply in Linguistic Inquiry 56(2)', 'contested'),
    ('Mongolian (reflexive possession)', 'Leaves no possessive and no chained clause anchorless: every "the" and every "and then" must declare whether it stays with the one already acting or hands over to another', 'Whether the one already acting stays the anchor', 'Language', 'Mongolic / reflexive-possessive reference tracking', 'The reflexive-possessive -AA binds a possessor or an embedded subject to the subject of the matrix clause, while the third-person possessive ni marks it as someone else; the same reflexive vs. non-reflexive contrast tracks reference across converb chains in Tungusic (Evenki, Ėven)', 'whether the one already acting stays the anchor', 'possessive', '["reflexive-possessive", "coreference", "reference-tracking", "switch-reference", "anchor"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Rank & salience"]', 'Janhunen, Mongolian (2012); Guntsetseg (2011), on reflexive possession and reference tracking', 'sourced'),
    ('Navajo (inalienable possession)', 'Prevents cognitively outsourcing system damage; problems are an inseparable part of one''s own system', 'Inseparability of system components', 'Language', 'Na-Dené / inalienable possession & animacy hierarchy', 'Strict distinction of alienable vs. inalienable possession; "I have a head" as a tool is impossible', 'whether the thing is separable from its owner', 'possessive', '["possession", "inalienable", "integral"]', '["Possession & belonging"]', 'Young & Morgan, The Navajo Language (1987); WALS ch. 58, Obligatory Possessive Inflection', 'sourced'),
    ('Navajo (classificatory shape verbs)', 'Couples the act to the geometry of what it takes hold of: there is no neutral "give" that leaves the object unclassified', 'The physical geometry of what is handled', 'Language', 'Na-Dené / classificatory verb stems by shape and handling', 'Verbs of handling, propelling and free flight take a different stem by the shape and consistency of the object — long and flexible, flat and elastic, round and compact, animate — so the verb cannot be chosen without first classifying the thing', 'the physical shape of the thing handled', 'verb', '["shape-verb", "geometry", "classificatory", "handling"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Young & Morgan, The Navajo Language: A Grammar and Colloquial Dictionary (1987)', 'sourced'),
    ('Cherokee (Tsalagi)', 'Forces permanent physical reality-checking: the state of aggregation must be carried syntactically', 'Physical geometry and consistency in space', 'Language', 'Iroquoian / classificatory handling verbs', 'No abstract "give"/"have"; the verb changes by whether the object is liquid, long-rigid, living, rope-like or a lump', 'the physical shape of the thing handled', 'verb', '["classificatory-verb", "consistency", "geometry"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Feeling & Pulte, Cherokee-English Dictionary (1975); Pulte, Outline of Cherokee Grammar', 'sourced'),
    ('Lakota (instrumental prefixes)', 'One root gives kabléča (shatter by a blow), nabléča (with the foot), yabléča (by biting), yubléča (with the hand): the event cannot be named before the force is named. Where Navajo and Cherokee vary the verb by the shape of the thing handled, this varies it by the energy applied, so a report cannot state that something broke and leave open what did the breaking', 'By which force a change of state came about', 'Language', 'Siouan / instrumental prefixes on bound verb roots', 'Seven frequent prefixes fix the means on the verb stem itself — ka- by a blow, na- by foot or by spontaneous inner force, pa- by pushing, wa- by a blade, wo- by piercing or shooting, ya- by mouth or teeth, yu- by hand — and for most of these stems no prefix-less form is in use', 'by which force the change came about', 'verb', '["instrumental", "force", "means", "bound-root", "causal"]', '["Causality"]', 'Rood & Taylor, Sketch of Lakhota, a Siouan Language (Handbook of North American Indians 17, 1996)', 'sourced'),
    ('Basque (Euskara)', 'Groups the actor of an intransitive clause with the undergoer of a transitive one, so a subject cannot claim agency from its position alone', 'System state instead of agency', 'Language', 'isolate / morphological ergativity', 'The ergative is licensed by the transitivity of the clause, not by the actor''s intent: a lone subject stays absolutive however deliberate the act (*etorri naiz* "I have come"), and unergatives take the ergative because they are syntactically transitive — a split that varies by dialect (*bazkaldu* ''to lunch'' is ergative in the west, absolutive in the east), which intent could not do — Hualde & Ortiz de Urbina', 'whether the participant patterns with the object', 'subject', '["ergative", "absolutive", "alignment", "transitivity"]', '["Agency & control"]', 'Hualde & Ortiz de Urbina, A Grammar of Basque', 'sourced'),
    ('Georgian (Kartuli)', 'Rules out the concept of the total, controlling ego', 'The human as a resonance space, not a causer', 'Language', 'South Caucasian / split-ergative & inversion', 'With psych verbs the syntax flips: not "I love her" but "she is mine as a beloved object" (I in the dative)', 'whether the experiencer is a causer or a site', 'subject', '["inversion", "ergative-split", "psych-verb"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Agency & control"]', 'Harris, Georgian Syntax (1981); Hewitt, Georgian: A Structural Reference Grammar (1995)', 'sourced'),
    ('Tagalog', 'Forces grasping the scenario flexibly from any perspective as the center', 'Dynamic center of the event', 'Language', 'Austronesian / symmetrical voice (focus) system', 'Neither active nor passive dominates; the verb determines which role (actor, object, place, instrument, beneficiary) is the lead', 'which participant the event is told from', 'verb', '["voice-system", "role", "focus-system"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Rank & salience"]', 'Schachter & Otanes, Tagalog Reference Grammar (1972)', 'sourced'),
    ('Hawaiian (a/o possession)', 'No "my" without deciding whether the thing hangs on me or I on it, so control and origin stop collapsing into one relation', 'Direction of dependency in every possessive relation', 'Language', 'Austronesian (Polynesian) / two possessive classes by direction of dependency', 'Two possessive classes: the a-class for what was acquired or is controlled (kaʻu puke — my book), the o-class for what one springs from or belongs to (koʻu makuahine — my mother, koʻu inoa — my name)', 'which side the dependency runs', 'possessive', '["possession", "a-o-class", "dependency", "control"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Agency & control"]', 'Elbert & Pukui, Hawaiian Grammar (1979)', 'sourced'),
    ('Pohnpeian (possessive classifiers)', 'Forces stating what the having is for, so "we have X" cannot stand as one undifferentiated relation', 'What the possessor means to do with the thing', 'Language', 'Micronesian (Oceanic) / possessive classifiers by intended use', 'More than twenty possessive classifiers select by the relation of use rather than by separability: kene for what is to be eaten, nime for what is to be drunk, with further classes for vehicles, dwellings, plantings and kin; the same noun takes another classifier when the intended use changes', 'what the having is for', 'possessive', '["possession", "possessive-classifier", "use-relation", "edible", "drinkable"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Object boundaries"]', 'Rehg & Sohl, Ponapean Reference Grammar (1981)', 'sourced'),
    ('Baniwa (attributive & privative)', 'Turns every attribution into a possession and forces its direction: what is said of a thing has to be stated as something the thing has or something it lacks, so a property cannot be predicated without naming the thing whose presence or absence it is', 'Every property as a presence or an absence of a thing', 'Language', 'Arawak (North Arawak) / attributive-privative prefix pair', 'Two prefixes derive a predicate from a noun and leave no third option: attributive ka- "having X" against privative ma- "lacking X" (ka-iipe > keepe "be fat", literally have-meat; Lokono ka-witi- "with eyes" against ma-witti- "blind"); both continue the Proto-Arawak attributive-privative pair and stay productive across the family', 'whether a property is a presence or an absence', 'noun', '["attributive", "privative", "ka-ma", "derivation", "presence-absence", "possession"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Aikhenvald, Classifiers in Multiple Environments: Baniwa of Içana/Kurripako; Carvalho, Reconstructing the Arawakan Privative', 'sourced'),
    ('Mojeño Trinitario (possessive classes)', 'Refuses the alienable/inalienable binary: a noun is obligatorily prefixed, optionally prefixed, or not prefixable at all, and moving between the classes costs derivation — so the question is not whether a thing has an owner but what kind of owning it admits', 'Which of three possessive classes a noun falls in', 'Language', 'Arawak (Southern Arawak) / three morphological possessive noun classes', 'Nouns fall into obligatorily prefixed, optionally prefixed and non-prefixable classes, the last needing derivation before a possessive prefix can attach at all; family-wide an obligatorily possessed noun that is to stand alone takes an unpossessed suffix reconstructed for Proto-Arawak (-tshi/-hV), so "a mouth" belonging to nobody is a derived form — Rose''s classes replace the alienable/inalienable split', 'which possessive class the noun belongs to', 'possessive', '["possession", "obligatory-possession", "unpossessed", "prefixability", "noun-class", "derivation"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Object boundaries"]', 'Rose, Questioning the Relevance of Alienability in Arawak Linguistics: An Innovative Analysis of Possession in Mojeño Trinitario, Linguistics 61(6) (2023)', 'sourced'),
    ('Dyirbal (noun classes)', 'Prevents viewing things in isolation', 'Cosmic and material belonging', 'Language', 'Pama-Nyungan / radical noun classes', 'Four noun classes (incl. "women, fire and dangerous things"); every noun is at once mythologically-functionally classified', 'which class the thing belongs to', 'noun', '["noun-classes", "classification"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Dixon, The Dyirbal Language of North Queensland (1972); Lakoff, Women, Fire, and Dangerous Things (1987)', 'sourced'),
    ('Dyirbal (syntactic ergativity)', 'Refuses to carry an actor through a chain of events unchanged: whoever is to remain the through-line must first be grammatically demoted to undergoer', 'Which participant may be the through-line of a clause chain', 'Language', 'Pama-Nyungan / absolutive pivot with antipassive repair', 'Clauses can only share an argument that is absolutive in each; to keep an actor as the shared topic the verb must be antipassivised (-ŋay), which demotes that actor into the absolutive — Dixon''s classic analysis', 'which participant may carry through the chain', 'subject', '["syntactic-ergativity", "pivot", "antipassive", "topic-chain", "voice"]', '["Rank & salience", "Agency & control"]', 'Dixon, The Dyirbal Language of North Queensland', 'sourced'),
    ('Ewe (logophoric pronouns)', 'A report cannot leave open whether it speaks of the one quoted or of a third party; the anchor of the perspective is carried on the pronoun itself', 'Anchor of the viewpoint in a report', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Kwa) / logophoric pronoun series', 'Inside reported speech a separate pronoun (yè) can refer only to the original speaker, while the ordinary pronoun in the same slot necessarily means someone else', 'whether a report speaks of the one quoted or another', 'person', '["logophoric", "reported-speech", "anchor", "perspective"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Evidence & certainty"]', 'Clements, The Logophoric Pronoun in Ewe: Its Role in Discourse, Journal of West African Languages 10 (1975), 141-177', 'sourced'),
    ('Ewe (body-part locatives)', 'Forces stating which part of a thing a relation attaches to, so "in the system" cannot stand without deciding whether the thing has an inside, a top or a back at all', 'Space as borrowed anatomy', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Kwa) / body-part nouns as the locative resource', 'Spatial relations run through body-part nouns projected onto the object instead of abstract prepositions: megbé (back) → behind it, ta (head) → on top of it, dome (belly) → inside or among', 'which part of the thing the relation attaches to', 'spatial frame', '["body-part-locative", "postposition", "anatomy", "space"]', '["Space & orientation", "Object boundaries"]', 'Ameka, on Ewe adpositions and body-part postpositions', 'sourced'),
    ('Wolof (focus conjugation)', 'No sentence without declaring where its weight lies; the participants stay untouched and only the emphasis is forced, which German carries invisibly in word order and stress', 'The highlighted element as a compulsory declaration', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Atlantic) / conjugation by focus rather than tense', 'The verbal complex is picked according to which element carries the focus — subject, predicate or complement — while tense rides along secondarily', 'where the weight of the sentence lies', 'verb', '["focus-conjugation", "information-structure", "emphasis", "highlighting"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Rank & salience"]', 'Robert, Le verbe wolof ou la grammaticalisation du focus; Torrence, The Clause Structure of Wolof (2013)', 'sourced'),
    ('Swahili (noun classes)', 'Reclassifies instead of classifying: moving a stem into another class re-sorts the entire sentence via concord', 'Mode of existence as a movable slot', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Bantu) / productive noun-class shifting', '15 to 18 noun classes whose agreement runs through the whole clause; the same stem is moved between classes: mtoto (child) becomes kitoto (small child) and utoto (childhood as a quality)', 'which mode of existence the thing is put in', 'noun', '["noun-classes", "concord", "reclassification", "derivation"]', '["Object boundaries", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Ashton, Swahili Grammar (Including Intonation) (1944)', 'sourced'),
    ('Luganda (graded tense)', 'Forces disclosing the distance of an event; a claim about today and one about a distant epoch cannot share a form', 'Distance of an event instead of its position on a time line', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Bantu) / graded temporal remoteness', 'Several distinct pasts and futures by remoteness (today / since yesterday / remote); the verb form states how far away an event lies, not merely that it lies behind', 'how far away the event lies', 'verb', '["remoteness", "graded-tense", "distance", "tense"]', '["Time & aspect"]', 'Ashton, Mulira, Ndawula & Tucker, A Luganda Grammar (1954)', 'sourced'),
    ('Khoekhoe (clusive we)', 'Nobody can say "we" without settling whether the person addressed stands inside or outside the group, and whether two or many are meant', 'Membership of the addressee in every "we"', 'Language', 'Khoe-Kwadi (areal "Khoisan") / person-gender-number markers with clusivity & dual', 'Person-gender-number markers attach to the nominal itself; the first person forces the choice between a "we" that includes the addressee and one that shuts them out, and carries its own dual beside the plural', 'whether the addressee is inside the group', 'person', '["clusivity", "inclusive-exclusive", "dual", "person-marking"]', '["Possession & belonging", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Hagman, Nama Hottentot Grammar (1977)', 'sourced'),
    ('Biblical Hebrew', 'Past and future are read off the state of the event more than off a time line', 'Processual status ahead of chronology', 'Language', 'Afro-Asiatic (Semitic) / aspect-dominant verbal system', 'The core opposition is aspectual rather than temporal: qatal presents the event as complete, yiqtol as incomplete, and neither is anchored to a past-present-future line. The classic account by which a prefixed waw *converts* a form into its opposite has been challenged — wayyiqtol is widely taken as a preserved narrative preterite from Proto-Semitic rather than an inverted yiqtol, and that reading is itself argued over; either way the language is less tenseless than the old description makes it', 'whether the event is completed or still running', 'verb', '["aspect", "perfect-imperfect", "waw"]', '["Time & aspect"]', 'Waltke & O''Connor, An Introduction to Biblical Hebrew Syntax (1990); Gesenius, Hebrew Grammar §49; on the wayyiqtol question: "Wayyiqtol: An Unlikely Preterite", Journal of Semitic Studies 58 (2013)', 'sourced'),
    ('Arabic (root & pattern)', 'Forces stating which derivation of a root is meant — causing, undergoing, seeking or doing mutually — instead of letting one word cover all of them', 'Derivational family instead of the isolated term', 'Language', 'Afro-Asiatic (Semitic) / root-and-pattern derivation', 'A triconsonantal root carries the semantic field; fixed templates derive the variants: kataba (write), kattaba (make write), kātaba (correspond with), takātaba (write to one another), istaktaba (ask to have written)', 'which derivation of the root the act is', 'verb', '["root-pattern", "derivation", "causative", "reciprocal"]', '["Causality", "Agency & control"]', 'Ryding, A Reference Grammar of Modern Standard Arabic (2005)', 'sourced'),
    ('Amharic (converb chains)', 'Forces deciding which single event of a sequence is actually asserted and which are demoted to accompaniment, instead of letting a row of equal main clauses suggest that everything is claimed alike', 'The one event that carries the assertion', 'Language', 'Afro-Asiatic (Ethiosemitic) / converb chains with a single finite anchor', 'Long chains of converbs run up to one closing finite verb; the converbs agree with their subject but carry no tense or mood, so only that last verb fixes when and how the whole chain happened', 'which event carries the assertion', 'connective', '["converb", "non-finite", "chaining", "anchor"]', '["Causality", "Coexistence"]', 'Leslau, Reference Grammar of Amharic (1995)', 'sourced'),
    ('Oromo (marked nominative)', 'Reverses the burden of marking: not being-affected but acting is the case that has to be signalled, so an agent cannot slip into the sentence unnoticed', 'The actor as the marked deviation, not the default', 'Language', 'Afro-Asiatic (Cushitic) / marked nominative', 'The nominative of the acting subject is the specially marked form while object and citation form stay bare — the reverse of the accusative pattern', 'whether the participant is the one acting', 'subject', '["marked-nominative", "case", "agent", "alignment"]', '["Agency & control", "Causality"]', 'König, Case in Africa (2008); WALS ch. 98, Alignment of Case Marking of Full Noun Phrases', 'sourced'),
    ('Hausa (pluractional verbs)', 'Separates how often something happened from how many were affected, so one act with many affected parties cannot pass as many separate acts', 'Number and distribution of the event, not of its participants', 'Language', 'Afro-Asiatic (Chadic) / pluractional verb stems', 'A reduplicated verb stem marks that an action happened repeatedly or separately to each participant: gyara (repair) becomes gyaggyara (repair many, repair again and again)', 'how often and to how many the event happened', 'verb', '["pluractional", "event-number", "distribution", "reduplication"]', '["Time & aspect", "Coexistence", "Object boundaries"]', 'Newman, The Hausa Language: An Encyclopedic Reference Grammar (2000); Newman, JALL 11 (1989)', 'sourced'),
    ('Ancient Greek (aorist & middle voice)', 'Separates the shape of an act from its length, and softens the actor-patient dualism by letting the subject stand inside its own act', 'Whether the subject stands inside its own act', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Hellenic) / aorist aspect & middle voice', 'The aorist presents the act as a single whole rather than as a process; in the indicative it carries the augment and does refer to past time, and only outside the indicative does it shed that reference. The middle voice puts the subject inside its own act — still the one acting, and the one the act comes back to', 'whether the subject is inside the act or outside it', 'verb', '["aorist", "middle-voice", "aspect"]', '["Time & aspect", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Smyth, A Greek Grammar for Colleges (1920)', 'sourced'),
    ('Ancient Greek (article & substantivization)', 'Lets a property, an activity or an entire statement be handled as a thing that can itself be predicated of and counted', 'Anything as a bounded object of thought', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Hellenic) / article-driven substantivization', 'The article nominalizes any element — adjective, participle, adverb, whole clause: τὸ ὄν (that which is), τὸ καλόν (the beautiful), τὰ νῦν (the present circumstances), τὸ ἐμὲ λέγειν (my speaking)', 'whether anything is being turned into a thing', 'noun', '["article", "substantivization", "nominalization", "abstraction"]', '["Object boundaries", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Smyth, A Greek Grammar for Colleges (1920)', 'sourced'),
    ('Ancient Greek (particles)', 'No step may stand unrelated, and the kind of link must be chosen; μέν … δέ forces every point to be given a counterweight before the next follows', 'Declared relation of every step to the previous one', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Hellenic) / obligatory discourse particles', 'Postpositive particles occupy a fixed second-position slot and name the link: μέν … δέ (paired counterweight), γάρ (ground), ἄρα (inference drawn), δή (as granted), μέντοι (concession)', 'which relation each step bears to the last', 'connective', '["particles", "discourse-relation", "counterweight", "concession"]', '["Causality", "Coexistence"]', 'Denniston, The Greek Particles (1934; 2nd ed. 1954)', 'sourced'),
    ('Latin (ablative absolute)', 'Separates the accompanying circumstance from the assertion while withholding the link, so the relation must be decided deliberately instead of slipping in as a "because"', 'Circumstance without a named relation', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Italic) / participial adjunct without a stated relation', 'A noun plus participle in the ablative hangs a whole circumstance beside the clause without saying whether it is cause, time, condition or concession (urbe capta — "the city having been taken")', 'whether the circumstance is cause, time, condition or concession', 'connective', '["ablative-absolute", "participle", "adjunct", "unstated-relation"]', '["Causality", "Coexistence"]', 'Pinkster, The Oxford Latin Syntax I-II (2015, 2021)', 'sourced'),
    ('Latin (gerundive of obligation)', 'Turns "I must do X" into "X is to be done", so what is required stands apart from whoever is responsible — and whether anyone is named at all becomes visible', 'Obligation as a property of the matter, not a decision of the doer', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Italic) / passive periphrastic with dative agent', 'The gerundive makes the task a predicate of the thing while the doer sinks into the dative: mihi liber legendus est ("the book is to-be-read by me"), and may be omitted entirely: delenda est Carthago', 'whether the obligation belongs to the matter or to a doer', 'subject', '["gerundive", "obligation", "dative-agent", "agentless"]', '["Agency & control"]', 'Pinkster, The Oxford Latin Syntax I-II (2015, 2021)', 'sourced'),
    ('Latin (oratio obliqua)', 'A claim taken over from someone else cannot be phrased like one''s own; the borrowed portion stays morphologically marked down to its last clause', 'Foreign claims marked as foreign across a whole passage', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Italic) / reported speech in infinitive & subjunctive', 'Reported content switches into accusative-plus-infinitive and its subordinate clauses into the subjunctive, for as long as the report runs (dixit urbem captam esse)', 'whether the passage is the speaker''s own claim', 'whole passage', '["oratio-obliqua", "reported-speech", "subjunctive", "attribution"]', '["Evidence & certainty", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Pinkster, The Oxford Latin Syntax I-II (2015, 2021)', 'sourced'),
    ('Hopi', 'Sets the degree of realization beside the time line rather than in place of it', 'Objective manifestation vs. subjective potential', 'Language', 'Uto-Aztecan / realis-irrealis reading of the -ni suffix', 'The -ni suffix separates what stands from what is still only intended, wished or expected; whether that is a future tense (Malotki) or a realis-irrealis mood (Comrie) is open, and only the second reading carries this lens', 'whether the content is manifested or still manifesting', 'verb', '["manifestation", "potential", "realization"]', '["Time & aspect", "Evidence & certainty"]', 'Malotki, Hopi Time (1983); realis/irrealis reading contra Malotki: Comrie', 'contested'),
    ('Russian', 'Hardly any action is expressible without committing to process or the final result', 'Fundamental nature of the result / manner of unfolding', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Slavic) / binary verbal aspect', 'Almost every verb as a pair: perfective (completed, goal-directed) and imperfective (ongoing, processual)', 'whether the act is goal-directed or processual', 'verb', '["aspect", "unfolding", "process"]', '["Time & aspect"]', 'Forsyth, A Grammar of Aspect (Cambridge, 1970); Comrie, Aspect (1976)', 'sourced'),
    ('Polish (instrumental predication)', 'Splits "X is Y" into two claims that cannot be conflated — occupying a category in some capacity, or having a property — so a definition cannot pass itself off as a description', 'Whether a predicate assigns a category or states a property', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Slavic) / case-marked predicate nouns', 'A predicate noun after "to be" goes into the instrumental, the case of "in the capacity of" (on jest nauczycielem — he is a teacher), while a predicate adjective stays nominative (on jest wysoki); the presenting construction to jest X keeps the nominative, so labelling and characterising cannot be the same sentence', 'whether the predicate assigns a category or a property', 'noun', '["case", "instrumental", "predication", "category", "copula"]', '["Object boundaries", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Swan, Polish Grammar in a Nutshell (2003)', 'sourced'),
    ('Polish (numeral threshold)', 'Forces a threshold at which counted individuals stop being a plurality and become an amount, so the affected and the figures cannot be the same kind of thing', 'Where a set of individuals turns into a quantity', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Slavic) / quantified phrases split at four', 'Two to four take the plural and agree as a plural subject (trzej chłopcy grali); from five on the counted noun stands in the genitive plural and the whole phrase takes third-person neuter singular agreement (pięć osób przyszło), so it is handled as one quantified mass rather than a set of individuals', 'where a set of individuals becomes a quantity', 'noun', '["numeral", "quantification", "threshold", "genitive", "agreement"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Swan, Polish Grammar in a Nutshell (2003)', 'sourced'),
    ('Polish (masculine-personal plural)', 'No group can be put in the plural without settling whether at least one man is among them, and one member''s property is projected onto the whole collective', 'Whether a group counts as containing a human male', 'Language', 'Indo-European (Slavic) / masculine-personal vs non-masculine-personal plural', 'Every plural splits into masculine-personal and everything else, and plural past-tense verbs, adjectives and pronouns follow the split: chłopcy byli / oni against kobiety, dzieci, psy and stoły były / one — women, children, animals and things share one form; the category resolves rather than counts, so one man among a thousand women flips the whole form', 'whether the group counts as containing a human male', 'noun', '["gender", "masculine-personal", "plural", "agreement", "resolution"]', '["Rank & salience", "Object boundaries"]', 'Swan, Polish Grammar in a Nutshell (2003)', 'sourced'),
    ('Finnish (partitive object)', 'Forces stating how far an action got through the case of what it touched, so an activity cannot be reported as a finished result without settling whether the thing was reached whole or only in part', 'Completion carried by the object, not the verb', 'Language', 'Uralic (Finnic) / partitive vs. total object', 'The object case decides how far the action got: luin kirjan (read the book through) against luin kirjaa (was reading at the book); an unbounded or partial quantity takes the same partitive (join veden / join vettä), and under negation every object turns partitive, so a denial cannot leave its object whole; Estonian marks the same contrast', 'how far the event reached the object', 'object', '["partitive", "total-object", "boundedness", "aspect", "case", "negation-scope"]', '["Time & aspect", "Object boundaries"]', 'Karlsson, Finnish: An Essential Grammar (Routledge)', 'sourced'),
    ('Hungarian (associative plural)', 'Separates a peer set from a focal element with its dependants before anything else is said about the group: three servers and the primary with its replicas are the same count and a different structure, and the plain plural cannot say the second', 'Whether a group is a set of equals or one member plus what belongs to it', 'Language', 'Uralic (Ugric) / associative plural', 'Beside the ordinary plural -k stands -ék: Jánosok is several people called János, Jánosék is János and the people belonging to him; naming a group forces the choice between the two', 'whether the group is equals or one member plus its belongings', 'noun', '["associative-plural", "plural", "grouping", "dependency", "salience"]', '["Coexistence", "Rank & salience"]', 'Daniel & Moravcsik, The Associative Plural (WALS ch. 36); Moravcsik (2003)', 'sourced'),
    ('Tuyuca', 'Prevents smuggling opinions in as facts', 'Verifiable origin of information', 'Language', 'Tucanoan / obligatory evidentiality', 'No sentence without one of five endings for the source: seen, heard, inference, hearsay, assumption', 'the source of the information', 'verb', '["evidentiality", "source", "verification"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'Barnes, Evidentials in the Tuyuca Verb, IJAL 50(3) (1984), 255-271', 'sourced'),
    ('Wintu', 'Leaves direct sight unmarked and makes every other route to the claim carry a suffix, so a departure from seeing cannot pass as seeing', 'Cognitive responsibility of the speaker', 'Language', 'Penutian (Wintuan) / four evidentials with plain sight as the unmarked default', 'Four suffixes mark the source — *-nthEr* nonvisual sensory, *-ke* hearsay, *-re* inferential, *-ʔel* assumed from experience of like cases; plain seeing carries no marker, so what has to be declared is every departure from it — Pitkin; Schlichter', 'the source of the information', 'verb', '["evidentiality", "source", "markedness", "nonvisual"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'Pitkin, Wintu Grammar (1984); Schlichter, The Origins and Deictic Nature of Wintu Evidentials (1986)', 'sourced'),
    ('Japanese', 'The agent-patient sentence becomes a mere statement about a state', 'Topic instead of agent', 'Language', 'topic-prominent language', 'wa topic marking separates the topic from the subject; agents can be dropped', 'what the sentence is about, apart from who acts', 'subject', '["topic", "wa", "agentless"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Rank & salience"]', 'Kuno, The Structure of the Japanese Language (1973); Shibatani, The Languages of Japan (1990)', 'sourced'),
    ('Japanese (listing particles)', 'A list cannot be handed over without declaring its own completeness. In requirements work the two particles separate "these are the stakeholders" from "these are stakeholders among others" — a difference German und leaves open and that surfaces later as a gap nobody agreed to', 'Whether an enumeration is closed or only exemplary', 'Language', 'Japonic / exhaustive vs. representative listing', 'Joining nouns forces a choice of particle: hon to pen names exactly the book and the pen and nothing else, hon ya pen names both as examples of a larger set that stays unspoken (often closed with nado); there is no neutral "and" to fall back on', 'whether the enumeration is closed or exemplary', 'connective', '["coordination", "listing", "exhaustive", "completeness", "particles"]', '["Coexistence", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Makino & Tsutsui, A Dictionary of Basic Japanese Grammar', 'sourced'),
    ('Yucatec Maya', 'Brings into view what counts as a bounded unit at all', 'Material instead of count', 'Language', 'classifier language', 'Nouns count as unbounded material; individuation is expressed via a classifier', 'whether the noun denotes a countable unit', 'noun', '["classifier", "material", "individuation"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Lucy, Grammatical Categories and Cognition (1992)', 'sourced'),
    ('Kayardild (modal case)', 'Stamps the tense, mood and polarity of the whole clause onto every non-subject nominal, so no participant can sit in a sentence temporally uninvolved', 'The temporal frame carried by every participant, not by the verb', 'Language', 'Tangkic (Australia) / case as a carrier of tense-mood-polarity', 'Nominals take up to four stacked case-like suffixes; one layer, Evans'' "modal case", is selected by the clause''s tense-mood-polarity, so a future or a negated clause re-inflects every non-subject noun and its modifiers; under "verbal case" nominals take verbal endings and agree in tense with the main verb while staying syntactically nominal', 'which temporal frame each participant stands in', 'noun', '["case-stacking", "modal-case", "polarity", "tam", "case"]', '["Time & aspect", "Coexistence"]', 'Evans, A Grammar of Kayardild (1995)', 'sourced'),
    ('Berik (diurnal tense)', 'Binds a statement to the light conditions under which the event happened, so nothing can be claimed in a flat, condition-free present', 'Position in the day-night cycle instead of on a time line', 'Language', 'Tor-Kwerba (Papua, Indonesia) / diurnal verbal marking', 'The verb carries whether the event fell in daylight or in darkness, bundled with the number and size of the object and the recipient: kitobana — "he gives three large objects to a male in the sunlight"', 'where in the day-night cycle the event fell', 'verb', '["diurnal", "cycle", "observation-conditions", "tense", "size"]', '["Time & aspect", "Object boundaries"]', 'A Grammatical Sketch of Berik, IRIAN XVI (1988), SIL', 'sourced'),
    ('Jingulu (three light verbs)', 'Forces every action to resolve into coming, going or neither, so no event can be named without settling its direction relative to a reference point', 'Direction as the obligatory core of every action', 'Language', 'Mirndi / West Barkly (Australia) / complex predicates on three inflecting verbs', 'Only three inflecting verbs — do, go, come; all further meaning sits in an uninflecting root while the light verb carries tense, mood, aspect and associated motion (come as approach, go as withdrawal, do as stasis)', 'which direction the act carries', 'verb', '["light-verb", "complex-predicate", "direction", "decomposition", "associated-motion"]', '["Space & orientation"]', 'Pensalfini, A Grammar of Jingulu (Pacific Linguistics)', 'sourced'),
    ('Tzeltal (uphill-downhill axis)', 'Forces every relation onto one fixed asymmetric gradient: two elements cannot be set beside each other without settling which of them lies further up the slope, so a flat side-by-side arrangement is not available', 'A fixed gradient of the land as the primary spatial axis', 'Language', 'Mayan / absolute terrain-based orientation', 'Space runs on ajk''ol (uphill), alan (downhill) and a residual across axis taken from the overall slope of the territory rather than from the visible local incline; no relative left/right frame is in systematic use — body-part terms exist, but they do not project a left and a right onto the world — and there is no cardinal set, and the axis is kept indoors, on flat ground and in rotated arrays — Brown and Levinson''s Tenejapa studies', 'whether space is reckoned from the speaker or from the world', 'spatial frame', '["terrain", "uphill-downhill", "absolute-orientation", "gradient", "landscape"]', '["Space & orientation"]', 'Brown & Levinson, Tenejapa Tzeltal studies', 'sourced'),
    ('Yoruba (serial verbs)', 'Refuses to let a chain be split into separately marked steps: one tense and one negation cover the whole series, so the links must be construable as a single event and their relations have to be inferred instead of being named by a "because" or an "in order to"', 'An event chain that has to hold as one single event', 'Language', 'Niger-Congo (Volta-Niger) / serial verb constructions', 'Several bare verbs share one subject and a single tense-aspect-negation specification with no linker between them: ó fi ọbẹ gé ẹran ("he took knife cut meat"), alongside comitative bá and directional or resultative series; which relation holds between the links — instrument, purpose, result, direction, beneficiary — is left unexpressed', 'whether the chain is one event or several', 'verb', '["serial-verb", "monoclausal", "linker-less", "tam-sharing", "instrument", "event-chain"]', '["Causality", "Coexistence"]', 'Baker & Stewart, A Serial Verb Construction without Constructions (on Yoruba)', 'sourced'),
    ('Mano (auxiliary series)', 'Denial cannot be graded as finely as assertion: what is claimed arrives already sorted by aspect and mood in the slot right after the subject, while what is refused loses that sorting and has to have it rebuilt further along the clause, so affirming and negating are not mirror images of one another', 'Assertion graded finely where denial is not', 'Language', 'Mande (South) / subject-indexing auxiliaries in TAMP series', 'Every clause runs S – AUX – (O) – V with an auxiliary that indexes the subject''s person and number and belongs to one of eleven series — perfect, past, existential, imperfective, conjoint, negative, conjunctive, prohibitive, subjunctive, prospective, dubitative — so the series is chosen in the second slot, before object and verb, while the full tense-aspect-mood-polarity value is carried by the construction as a whole; some series are told apart by tone alone (lɛ́ɛ̀ 3sg.ipfv against lɛ̀ɛ́ 3sg.neg), past auxiliaries and the negative particle fuse with a third person singular object (ē 3sg.pst against ā 3sg.pst>3sg), and a single negative series covers the negative perfective, imperfective, imperfect, future and irrealis alike, the distinctions it drops being rebuilt by particles and verb forms elsewhere in the clause — gbā for the perfective, nɛ́ŋ̀ "not yet" for the perfect, the auxiliary kɛ̄ plus an infinitive for the imperfect; Khachaturyan''s Mano grammar', 'which grade of assertion the clause carries', 'verb', '["auxiliary-series", "tam", "polarity", "person-marking", "negation-asymmetry", "portmanteau", "tone"]', '["Time & aspect", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Khachaturyan, Grammaire du mano (2015)', 'sourced'),
    ('Ithkuil', 'Forces a mathematical triage of one''s own thought', 'Elimination of ambiguity; hyper-precise categorization', 'Constructed language', 'philosophically constructed (a posteriori/a priori)', 'One word-complex encodes multi-layered logical distinctions; forces stating whether a consequence is intended, unintended, physically forced or psychologically conditioned', 'whether the consequence was intended, forced or conditioned', 'verb', '["constructed", "precision", "triage"]', '["Logic & ambiguity", "Causality"]', 'Quijada, Ithkuil (published by the author, 2004)', 'sourced'),
    ('Lojban', 'Makes an unintended reading syntactically impossible: every argument place is filled or explicitly left open, and the parse is unique', 'First-order predicate logic as syntax', 'Constructed language', 'logically constructed language (loglang)', 'No split between noun and verb; everything predicates; syntactically free of ambiguity, parseable like formulas', 'whether the statement parses without ambiguity', 'whole passage', '["logic", "predicate", "unambiguous"]', '["Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Cowan, The Complete Lojban Language (Logical Language Group, 1997)', 'sourced'),
    ('Toki Pona', 'Acts like a radical word ban against bureaucratic obfuscation', 'Radical reduction to the essence of the moment', 'Constructed language', 'minimalist constructed (philosophical game)', 'Only ~120–137 words; breaks overloaded concepts into primitive blocks ("car" = "moving container")', 'what survives a vocabulary of a hundred words', 'whole passage', '["minimalism", "reduction", "word-ban"]', '["Object boundaries"]', 'Lang, Toki Pona: The Language of Good (2014)', 'sourced'),
    ('Turkish (mirative -mIş)', 'Separates what a speaker has long held from what has just reached them, so a fact cannot be delivered in the register of the settled while it is still arriving', 'Whether the speaker had already taken the fact in', 'Language', 'Turkic / mirative marking of unintegrated information', '-mIş sits in the same slot as the past -DI and marks that the speaker''s mind was not prepared for what is said: the proposition is new to them, not yet built into what they hold (DeLancey, on Slobin & Aksu''s Turkish data). Whether this is a category of its own or a use of evidentiality is argued over; what the marker does is not', 'whether the content is new to the speaker', 'verb', '["mirativity", "unexpected", "new-information", "surprise"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'DeLancey, Mirativity: The Grammatical Marking of Unexpected Information, Linguistic Typology 1 (1997); Slobin & Aksu on Turkish -mIş', 'sourced'),
    ('Kathmandu Newar (conjunct-disjunct)', 'Makes the right to assert part of the verb: the same event told by the one who did it and by someone who watched cannot take the same form', 'The standing from which something may be said', 'Language', 'Tibeto-Burman / egophoric verb forms', 'The verb form turns on whether the speaker is the participant with privileged access to the event: the conjunct form where they are, the disjunct elsewhere. The split follows epistemic authority and involvement rather than grammatical person, which is why it survives into questions with the addressee as its pivot — Hale''s conjunct/disjunct', 'whether the speaker has privileged access to what is said', 'verb', '["egophoricity", "conjunct-disjunct", "epistemic-authority", "access", "involvement"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'Hale, Person Markers: Finite Conjunct and Disjunct Verb Forms in Newari (1980); Hargreaves, "Am I Blue?": Privileged Access Constraints in Kathmandu Newar; Widmer & Zúñiga on egophoricity against evidentiality', 'sourced'),
    ('Amele (switch-reference)', 'Puts reference tracking into the seam between clauses: a chain cannot be built without stating at every step whether the actor carries over, so a silent change of subject is not available', 'Whether the next step keeps the same doer', 'Language', 'Trans-New Guinea / switch-reference on medial verbs', 'Every medial verb of a clause chain carries a same-subject or different-subject marker before the chain closes on its one finite verb, so continuity of the participant is declared at the joint instead of being inferred across it; the same device runs in Seri and Kiowa', 'whether the next clause keeps this subject', 'connective', '["switch-reference", "same-subject", "different-subject", "clause-chaining", "reference-tracking"]', '["Rank & salience", "Coexistence"]', 'Haiman & Munro (eds.), Switch-Reference and Universal Grammar (Typological Studies in Language 2, 1983)', 'sourced'),
    ('Spanish (differential object marking)', 'Refuses to treat the object slot as uniform: a person and a thing in the same role do not get the same form, so what was affected cannot be asked without asking what sort of thing it was', 'What kind of thing is allowed to fill the object slot unmarked', 'Language', 'Romance / object marked by animacy and definiteness', 'A direct object takes the preposition *a* only when it stands high enough on animacy and definiteness — *veo a Juan* against *veo la casa*. The same grammatical role is marked or bare depending on what fills it; Aissen derives the split from a standing tension between iconicity and economy', 'whether the object is individuated enough to be marked', 'object', '["differential-object-marking", "animacy", "definiteness", "case", "individuation"]', '["Object boundaries", "Rank & salience"]', 'Aissen, Differential Object Marking: Iconicity vs. Economy, Natural Language & Linguistic Theory 21 (2003)', 'sourced'),
    ('Hungarian (preverbal focus position)', 'Makes exhaustive identification a matter of position rather than of stress: a sentence cannot be built without deciding whether anything is being singled out at all, and if so which', 'Which single constituent the sentence singles out', 'Language', 'Uralic (Ugric) / structural focus in the immediately preverbal slot', 'The slot immediately before the verb is a structural focus position: whatever stands there is read as identificational focus and carries exhaustivity — it names the one that holds and excludes the rest, as an English cleft does. Leaving the slot unfilled is equally a decision', 'which constituent occupies the position before the verb', 'word order', '["focus", "exhaustivity", "preverbal", "information-structure", "position"]', '["Rank & salience"]', 'É. Kiss, The Syntax of Hungarian (Cambridge); É. Kiss, Identificational Focus versus Information Focus, Language 74 (1998)', 'sourced');

