    ('Skaldic Dróttkvætt', 'Leaves nothing to the ear''s discretion: whether a line takes the half-rhyme or the full one is settled before a word is chosen, by whether it is the odd line or the even one', 'The rhyme the line''s own position prescribes', 'Poetic form', 'Old Norse skaldic / complex stanza meter', 'Six syllables per line, and the internal rhyme is fixed by line parity: odd lines take skothending (half-rhyme, differing vowels over identical following consonants), even lines aðalhending (full rhyme, both matching), with kennings carrying the nouns', 'which rhyme the line''s position demands', 'surface', '["skaldic", "internal-rhyme", "skothending", "adalhending", "line-parity"]', '[]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012)', 'sourced'),
    ('Minnesang (canzone form)', 'The Stollen light two facets of the same deficit, the Abgesang breaks the structure onto a new level', 'Dialectical verification through broken symmetry; relationship/reciprocity', 'Poetic form', 'Middle High German canzone form (AAB)', 'Two identically built Stollen (Aufgesang) and a differently structured Abgesang (AAB); wooing I-you orientation', 'whether the symmetry breaks where the address turns', 'whole passage', '["stollen-abgesang", "broken-symmetry", "dialogic"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012)', 'sourced'),
