    ('Rondo', 'Prevents cognitive drift: after each analytical idea the form forces a return to the opening statement', 'Permanent return to the core problem after each excursion', 'Narrative structure', 'musical form adopted for text / refrain against episodes (ABACA, ABACABA)', 'A fixed refrain (A) returns unchanged between ever-new episodes (B, C, D), so each excursion is measured against the same statement rather than against the one before it', 'which core the excursions keep returning to', 'whole passage', '["refrain", "return", "focus", "music"]', '[]', 'Grove, A Dictionary of Music and Musicians, art. "Rondo"', 'sourced'),
    ('Rondeau', 'Cuts a phrase back until it still carries on its own, then tests the cut twice against material that arrived later — what survives was never leaning on its sentence', 'What a fragment of the opening can carry alone', 'Poetic form', 'French forme fixe / thirteen or fifteen lines on two rhymes with a rentrement', 'The rentrement is not a whole line but the first few words of line 1, and it returns as the closing line of the second and third stanzas (aabba aabR aabbaR); a fragment torn out of a sentence has to work as an ending twice, in contexts written after it', 'which fragment of the opening can stand as a closing line', 'whole passage', '["rentrement", "refrain", "fragment", "forme-fixe", "compression"]', '[]', 'Greene et al., The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012), art. "rondeau"', 'sourced'),
    ('Canso', 'The first sentence determines the phonetic fate of all others; thoughts are subordinated to the acoustic form', 'Acoustic pre-programming of subsequent thoughts', 'Poetic form', 'troubadour poetry / coblas with tornada', 'The rhymes of the first stanza dictate the vowels/consonants of all following stanzas; often a closing tornada', 'which sounds the first stanza commits the rest to', 'surface', '["coblas", "rhyme", "sound-constraint"]', '[]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012)', 'sourced'),
