    ('Plato''s Allegory of the Cave', 'Forces viewing current measurements as mere "shadows on the wall" and seeking the true problem outside the familiar surface', 'Epistemic level shift', 'Narrative structure', 'allegorical structure / three-stage', 'Shadow paradise (deception), ascent (insight/pain), return (incomprehension of the crowd)', 'which level of knowledge the account is told from', 'whole passage', '["allegory", "insight", "levels"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'Plato, Republic VII, 514a-520a', 'sourced'),
    ('Frame narrative', 'Each symptom becomes its own sub-narrative with its own rules and encapsulates complexity modularly', 'Recursive nesting of meta-levels', 'Narrative structure', 'nesting (e.g. One Thousand and One Nights)', 'An outer narrative brackets an inner one, which in turn begets yet another', 'how many levels of telling stand between reader and event', 'whole passage', '["frame", "recursion", "nesting"]', '["Evidence & certainty"]', 'Genette, Discours du récit (1972), on narrative levels', 'sourced'),
    ('Mise en abyme', 'Separates the merely nested from the self-repeating: unlike a frame bracketing a different story, here the inner level carries the same structure, so whatever holds of it must hold of the whole', 'The detail that repeats the whole', 'Narrative structure', 'heraldic device adopted for literature (Gide) / the part reproduces the whole', 'An element inside the work reproduces the structure of the work itself — the picture that contains the picture, the story whose inner story is its own', 'which detail repeats the whole', 'whole passage', '["mise-en-abyme", "self-similarity", "reflexivity", "levels"]', '["Rank & salience"]', 'Dällenbach, Le récit spéculaire: essai sur la mise en abyme (1977); Gide, Journal (1893)', 'sourced'),
    ('Dialectical Midrash', 'The problem must not be harmonized; it demands letting incompatible analyses stand side by side', 'Multi-perspective ambiguity tolerance', 'Narrative structure', 'rabbinic exegesis / preserved disagreement on one page (Mikraot Gedolot, Talmud)', 'A core text is surrounded by independent, partly contradictory commentaries that all hold simultaneously', 'which contradictory readings hold at once', 'whole passage', '["exegesis", "contradiction", "commentary"]', '["Logic & ambiguity", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Mikraot Gedolot (the Rabbinic Bible): a core text with simultaneous, disagreeing commentaries; Carasik, The Commentators'' Bible', 'sourced'),
    ('Vedic Hymn (Rigveda)', 'Forces analyzing the "fault" in its functional role for the whole system (what does it involuntarily stabilize?)', 'Acceptance of the ambivalence of destructive forces', 'Narrative structure', 'Rigveda structure / hymnic enumeration', 'Enumeration of complementary cosmic forces that condition and destroy each other (Agni destroys and purifies)', 'which complementary forces condition each other', 'whole passage', '["hymn", "ambivalence", "forces"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Jamison & Brereton, The Rigveda (Oxford, 2014), with the online commentary', 'sourced'),
    ('Koan', 'Deliberately crashes rational SVO thinking to force intuitive insight beyond true/false', 'Short-circuiting the logical-analytical mind', 'Narrative structure', 'Zen Buddhism / paradox', 'A rationally unsolvable paradox ("What is the sound of one hand clapping?")', 'which question the analytical route cannot answer', 'whole passage', '["paradox", "zen", "intuition"]', '["Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Britannica, art. "Koan", on the rationally insoluble paradox in Rinzai practice', 'sourced'),
    ('Fugue', 'Rules out treating the counter-position as a later chapter: statement and answer are developed in the same pass rather than in sequence, and the stretto tests whether the answer still holds when it arrives before the statement is complete', 'The counter-voice that has to run at the same time, not afterwards', 'Narrative structure', 'musical form adopted for text / dux answered by comes over a countersubject', 'A theme (dux) is stated and answered by a second voice a fifth away (comes) while the first voice keeps going against it as countersubject; the material may return inverted, augmented or diminished, and in the stretto the entries overlap before any voice has finished', 'which counter-voice runs at the same time', 'whole passage', '["counterpoint", "dux-comes", "simultaneity", "inversion", "stretto"]', '["Coexistence", "Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Grove, A Dictionary of Music and Musicians, art. "Fugue"', 'sourced'),
    ('Incantatio', 'Phrase the problem as a binding decree ("I bind variable X to state Y") instead of a report; turns observation into responsibility', 'Radical assumption of intentional agency', 'Technique', 'Roman spell / performative speech act', 'Rigid rhythmic alliteration (carmen) meant to bind reality by being spoken', 'whether the saying is meant to bind', 'whole passage', '["performative", "decree", "agency"]', '["Agency & control"]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012); Cato, De Agri Cultura CLX, for a carmen preserved in use', 'sourced'),
    ('Cento', 'Bans your own words: foreign manuals, bug reports or quotes must be recombined until your own truth becomes visible', 'Pure recombination out of the deficit', 'Technique', 'Roman patchwork poem / pure recombination', 'The text consists solely of copied lines/phrases from other authors', 'which borrowed lines the whole is built from', 'whole passage', '["collage", "recombination", "foreign-quote"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity"]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012)', 'sourced'),
    ('Lipogram', 'Describing without certain words/letters fires areas that must lay entirely new conceptual paths', 'Circumventing a synaptic blockade', 'Technique', 'antiquity / Oulipo / letter ban', 'A given letter (e.g. "e") is banned throughout the text', 'which letter the text does without', 'surface', '["oulipo", "letter-ban", "constraint"]', '[]', 'Greene et al. (eds.), The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012)', 'sourced'),
    ('S+7 (N+7)', 'Collapses the practised vocabulary of a field so only the relations between the slots remain: whatever still reads as an argument was carried by the structure, not by the terminology. Needs a fixed word list — without one the substitution is invented instead of counted and the result is not reproducible', 'The structural skeleton that stays readable once the familiar terms are gone', 'Technique', 'Oulipo (Lescure, 1961) / dictionary-based noun substitution', 'Every noun is replaced by the seventh noun following it in one named dictionary, counted mechanically, while syntax and function words stay untouched', 'what the frame reads like with the nouns displaced', 'noun', '["oulipo", "substitution", "renaming", "constraint", "word-list"]', '[]', 'Lescure, Oulipo (1961)', 'sourced'),
    ('Clinamen', 'Perec''s knight''s tour across the 10x10 building skips one square, which is why the book has 99 chapters and not 100. Name the rule a system imposes on itself, break it once at a point where compliance was still available, and read off what the exception costs: a rule whose breach changes nothing was never holding anything up', 'Whether a rule carries anything or is decoration', 'Technique', 'Oulipo (Perec, La Vie mode d''emploi) / one deliberate breach of one''s own constraint', 'A strict constraint is set up and then broken exactly once, on purpose; the Oulipo condition is that the breach may only be taken where the rule could still have been followed', 'where the rule is broken on purpose', 'whole passage', '["clinamen", "oulipo", "constraint", "exception", "broken-symmetry"]', '["Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Perec, La Vie mode d''emploi', 'sourced'),
    ('Enumeration', 'Refuses the causal chain structurally and forces seeing things as coexisting rather than emerging from one another', 'Coexistence instead of causality', 'Register', 'catalogue / listing without predicating verbs', 'Works without direction-asserting verbs', 'whether the parts need a direction-asserting verb', 'connective', '["coordination", "coexistence", "states"]', '["Causality", "Coexistence"]', 'Greene et al., The Princeton Encyclopedia of Poetry and Poetics (4th ed., 2012), art. "catalog"', 'sourced'),
    ('Back-translation', 'Translate a passage into another language and back; what breaks was language-bound, what survives counts as more robust', 'Test the robustness of a formulation', 'Technique', 'thinking tool', 'Exposes language-bound idiosyncrasies via the detour through a foreign language', 'what does not survive a detour through another language', 'whole passage', '["translation", "detour", "robustness"]', '[]', 'Brislin, Back-Translation for Cross-Cultural Research, Journal of Cross-Cultural Psychology 1(3) (1970)', 'sourced'),
    ('Form-switch', 'Forces the same content into a foreign genre (a proof as a recipe, a process as a map), so connections the usual form concealed surface', 'Make concealed connections visible', 'Technique', 'thinking tool', 'A different genre forces different connections', 'which connections another genre forces', 'whole passage', '["genre", "transformation", "reframe"]', '[]', 'Queneau, Exercices de style (1947)', 'sourced'),
    ('Word ban', 'Bans a single word for a paragraph (because, leads to, any directional verb); whatever remains and holds was never dependent on the hierarchy', 'Test dependence on the causal structure', 'Technique', 'thinking tool / E-Prime lineage (general semantics)', 'A targeted word ban exposes unnecessary hierarchy. The lineage is E-Prime, which bans every form of *to be* to expose hidden identity claims — not Oulipo, whose constraints on this axis are letter-level (lipogram, prisoner''s constraint) and whose one word-level device, S+7, substitutes rather than forbids', 'what the passage says without one named word', 'whole passage', '["word-ban", "constraint", "causal"]', '["Causality", "Agency & control"]', 'Bourland, A Linguistic Note: Writing in E-Prime, General Semantics Bulletin (1965); Korzybski, Science and Sanity (1933)', 'sourced'),
    ('Perspective swap', 'Recounts the same event from the viewpoint of the supposed object, so the ranking of actor and patient dissolves', 'Dissolve the ranking of agent and object', 'Technique', 'thinking tool', 'Shifts the viewpoint onto the grammatical object', 'what the account looks like from the object', 'subject', '["perspective", "object", "ranking"]', '["Perspective & reciprocity", "Rank & salience"]', 'Genette, Discours du récit (1972)', 'sourced'),
    ('Scale test', 'Tests a claimed self-similarity instead of asserting it: whatever forces its own vocabulary at one level is the place where the fractal analogy breaks and something genuinely new begins', 'Whether a level of a system has a description of its own', 'Technique', 'thinking tool', 'The description is written once and then required to hold at every level of the system; wherever a level needs vocabulary of its own, the self-similarity ends there', 'whether every level of the system has its own description', 'whole passage', '["self-similarity", "scale-invariance", "levels", "recursion", "constraint"]', '["Rank & salience", "Coexistence"]', 'Anderson, More Is Different, Science 177(4047) (1972)', 'sourced'),
    ('Node collapse', 'Inverts the argument-slot logic: instead of filling places with things it empties them, so a statement about entities has to show what in it is more than the relations it asserts', 'What survives when the things are replaced by their relations', 'Technique', 'thinking tool / structuralist and structural-realist reduction', 'Every noun is rewritten as the bundle of relations it stands in until only the net remains; whatever cannot be dissolved is read off as the actual claim', 'what survives when the things become their relations', 'noun', '["relational", "structuralism", "node-free", "reduction", "residue"]', '["Object boundaries", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Worrall (1989); Ladyman (1998), ontic structural realism', 'sourced'),
    ('Vigraha (compound resolution)', 'Forces the silent relation inside a compound to be spoken, so a compound cannot occupy an argument slot where a clause with named participants would be required; whatever resolves into a phrase with a missing member is a claim with a hole in it', 'Which case relation the members of a compound hide', 'Technique', 'Sanskrit grammar (Pāṇinian) / analytic resolution of a samāsa', 'Every compound is rewritten as the phrase it abbreviates, with the relation between its members restored as an explicit case: a tatpuruṣa names the case its first member stands in (rāja-puruṣa → rājñaḥ puruṣaḥ, "the king''s man"), a bahuvrīhi points outside itself to a possessor it never names, a dvandva coordinates equals; the class cannot be assigned without naming the relation', 'which case relation the compound hides', 'noun', '["compound", "samasa", "vigraha", "decomposition", "relation", "case"]', '["Object boundaries", "Logic & ambiguity"]', 'Pāṇini, Aṣṭādhyāyī', 'sourced');
